# Python
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# Entorno local
.env

# Harness logs (ephemeral, regenerated)
.logs/

# Tracker local de /wayfinder
.scratch/

# Per-machine Claude Code state. The skills and agents ARE committed - see below.
.claude/settings.local.json

# `.mcp.json` stays out, and this one is not a judgement call: it hardcodes absolute
# paths from the machine it was generated on (`/home/<user>/.nvm/.../npx`) plus a
# `framework` binary this repository no longer depends on. It would not work anywhere
# else, so committing it would be committing something broken.
.mcp.json

# `.claude/agents/` IS committed: those four are the adversarial reviewers README's "AI in
# My Workflow" describes, and a document that names a gate whose artifact is absent from
# the repository is asking to be taken on trust.
#
# `.claude/skills/` is committed too, but holds only what is genuinely about this project.
# The workflow's own rules - the logging contract, the Context7 gate, the visual-evidence
# contract - are personal tooling that lives outside this repository; what the README
# points at instead is what they produced: app/observability.py, .logs/agent/,
# scripts/logcheck.py.
#
# Nothing here carries an absolute path or a secret. `.dockerignore` keeps `.claude/` out
# of the image, which is where "development tooling never ships" is actually enforced.
